ncreasing local food  production with modern Technologies  so we're working on that was very  interesting to listen to The Working  session today in this college to college  meeting together with our African  Partners to make the Food Systems more  resilient  and more self-sufficient  I think there is an enormous potential  listening to The Experts to scale up the  food production in Africa and mostly to  have more processing on the continent so  that there is value added to the product  from cashew to cacao there are many  examples where this should be possible  um and of course a second important  topic in the food security element is  that we are investing in Innovative  agricultural processes like for example  the next generation of fertilizers  so we want to work together on Solutions  here to make fertilizers in Africa not  only accessible but also affordable  there are many topics that we touched on  in the field of the fertilizers  bottlenecks where you have to work on  problems that we solved and with that  experience that it's not very not  one-dimensional but multi-dimensional we  agreed today to launch a Joint Task  Force on fertilizers to tackle together  the various challenges for the short and  for the long term what fertilizers are  concerned  second big topic is of course the energy  crisis  Russia's blackmail with energy was an  eye-opener for us the European Union  and we are not only diversifying our  energy  to the energy demand to Reliable  suppliers but we are massively  accelerating the clean energy transition  and I think this is a defining moment  where we can join forces  Africa has abundant resources for clean  energy  and the shift to clean energy if we do  it right could provide access for  Africans to clean energy that is  homegrown and that is Affordable  this needs investment and you can count  on our support to make this happen  we are already mobilizing massive  funding for energy projects from solar  to hydropower to interconnectors  but in addition to that  I think this energy transition might  bring new economic opportunities to  Africa because the world needs renewable  energy and Africa could become a major  exporter of renewable energy to the  world  we have already together taken the very  first steps by assigning renewable  energy Partnerships for example with  Namibia for example with Egypt in the  margins of cop 27 and more will follow  thirdly we discussed about the  implementation of our joint priorities  we have already a common agenda which we  agreed last February at The aueu Summit  and we have a tool to implement it that  is our global gateway investment plan  for Africa worth 150 billion euros  and here it is our common approach to  define the mature projects that are  important  we are partially already delivering with  decisive infrastructure infrastructure  projects I have already mentioned the  energy projects everyone knows our  vaccine projects the vaccine projects  are very impressive because they show  exemplary what we have to combine in  such Investments it's public money it's  private money technology has to go to  the continent and skills have to be  developed locally this is so important  of course there's also the very  important topic of Transport we've  recently inaugurated a highway along the  northern Corridor and Kenya  um and work should start soon on  submarine cables to digitally connect  eastern and western Africa with Europe  so these are many different examples but  um they show the direction of travel  for these projects we are ready to sign  a new financing agreement with 750  million euros on clean energy on  strategic transport corridors and on  digitalization  my final takeaway from our afternoon  dear Musa is that we are delivering on  our agenda despite the turbulent  geopolitical situation we see and I  think we can achieve even more because  we share many objectives for example the  necessary reforms in WTO and the UN  system or the debt relief for vulnerable  countries you have our full support  of course the necessity to fight climate  change  and this is a reason why I fully support  Africa's ambition to play a stronger  role in the multilateral system  including the G20  and at the cop conference for the  climate so you're right  um to insist on that  it is good that the cop 27s took a step  towards climate Justice with loss and  damage  and you can count on the European Union  to help make it operational  and of course in that context we expect  all major emitters to contribute to loss  and damage and climate adaptation  we will keep on working on this and many  more projects and I'm very much looking  forward to take stock the next time when  we are visiting this college is visiting  your college in Addis abiba thank you  very much  [Music]  foreign  continue  the new deliberation  is  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  foreign  ER  locations  [Music]  important  terrorism is Mozambique  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  Continental  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  purity  is I say see some million Africa some  prevent electricity  a donkey  in the necessity  transition energetic business  foreign  [Music]  energy  the security Elementor  Treasures  that is  [Music]  [Music]  Ukraine  mathematic  pose an African beautiful here and  ground problem  s  is  foreign  foreign  [Music]  travel  [Music]  hello problematicism  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  merci beaucoup we will now take a few  questions of course we have the  journalists present in the room but just  for Mr chairperson to no we also have  Johnny's connected remotely so I will  also give the photo to some of those if  they wish to take the floor so yes  please go ahead  very much and thank you for having this  chance to exchange on this this topic  after February uh my name is Vince  Chadwick I'm a journalist with devex in  Brussels  um I wanted to ask a question to um  president wonderlaim so much of the  European commission's political energy  goes into the EU Au relationship and we  see that today but it's another African  presence in Brussels called the oacps  the organization of African Caribbean  Pacific States who uh have just lost one  of their biggest members in South Africa  and EU taxpayers pay millions of dollars  to this organization every year but  whenever Europe talks to Africa it's  often with the Au and I wondered what  your explanation and justification is to  your European citizens who are  contributing to the eu's relationship  with the ACP when even South Africa is  now leaving the ACP and the signature of  the post College New agreement is stuck  for various reasons notably being  blocked by Hungary  is it time now to turn the page  on the post-continue agreement to not  sign it to move on and to come up with a  new way to relate to the Pacific and the  Caribbean and to find this forum is the  home for a logical measured accountable  transparent relationship with Africa so  what's the point of the post-continue  agreement today it's basically my  question and to chairperson faki I  wanted to  ask you you said um  that the relationship with Europe is  going well  um obviously it's a short period of time  February to November  what concrete deliverables can you point  to that you welcome that have happened  since February in the summit and and now  what what's going well and what's not  going well in your view thank you  so I think it's always worse to invest  um strength and time into relationships  that have been established whether they  go through difficult times or whether we  are in good times and of course beyond  that we do have also other bilateral  relationship to the pacifics and the  Caribbean so topics where we're working  together are also on the agenda so I  would not want to give up on that  Africa  and please  don't foreign  security  system  [Music]  for example  for example on so many  uh the finance  principle the another mission  transformation  it is  [Music]  